               found that a reward led to a repetition of the previous choice,
               whereas a penalty led to an avoidance of the previous choice.
               Surprisingly, the effects of the reward magnitude and the penalty
               magnitude revealed a pronounced asymmetry. The choice repetition
               effect of a reward scaled with the magnitude of the reward. In a
               marked contrast, the avoidance effect of a penalty was flat, not
               influenced by the magnitude of the penalty. These effects were
               mechanistically described using a reinforcement learning model
