  publisher = "Elsevier BV",
  volume    =  41,
  pages     = "122--127",
  abstract  = "A variety of behavioral and neural phenomena suggest that
               organisms evaluate outcomes not on an absolute utility scale, but
               relative to some dynamic and context-sensitive reference or
               scale. Sometimes, as in foraging tasks, this results in sensible
               choices; in other situations, like choosing between options
               learned in different contexts, irrational choices can result. We
               argue that what unites and demystifies these various phenomena is
               that the brain's goal is not assessing utility as an end in
